WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.411
Session guarantees describe what one client may
observe across requests.

00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:08.639
They are more precise than saying that data is
eventually consistent.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:14.643
Follow one inspection report as its reader changes
replicas, and name the guarantee broken at each

00:00:14.643 --> 00:00:14.949
step.

00:00:14.949 --> 00:00:21.076
A field technician saves inspection version twelve
and then reads the report through another replica

00:00:21.076 --> 00:00:23.343
that has reached only version eleven.

00:00:23.343 --> 00:00:26.079
Returning eleven violates read-your-writes.

00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:32.188
Later, after the technician has seen version
thirteen, returning twelve violates monotonic reads

00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:36.006
even if twelve includes the technician’s own earlier
change.

00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:40.206
These guarantees protect different parts of the
user’s experience.

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:43.515
One design attaches a progress token to the session.

00:00:43.515 --> 00:00:49.815
A chosen replica serves the read only after reaching
the required progress, or the request moves to

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:51.024
a suitable replica.

00:00:51.043 --> 00:00:57.147
In this simplified single-record example the token
can be a version; multi-partition systems need

00:00:57.147 --> 00:00:58.154
richer tracking.

00:00:58.154 --> 00:01:03.440
Pinning a session can reduce switches but does not
by itself handle replica failure.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:09.732
Waiting a fixed number of milliseconds is also
insufficient because replication delay is not
bounded

00:01:09.732 --> 00:01:10.613
by that timer.

00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:14.497
Quorum diagrams help us reason about which copies
must meet.

00:01:14.497 --> 00:01:19.123
The arithmetic is useful precisely when we keep its
assumptions visible.

00:01:19.123 --> 00:01:25.419
Work through a five-replica example, then separate
set intersection from the stronger guarantee we

00:01:25.419 --> 00:01:25.997
may want.

00:01:25.997 --> 00:01:28.438
A record has five designated replicas.

00:01:28.438 --> 00:01:34.027
A write waits for acknowledgements from three, and a
read obtains responses from three.

00:01:34.043 --> 00:01:39.083
Every possible pair of those sets overlaps because
three plus three exceeds five.

00:01:39.083 --> 00:01:44.871
If two replicas are unreachable, the remaining three
can still supply the required responses,

00:01:44.871 --> 00:01:49.724
assuming they are mutually reachable and the
protocol’s other conditions hold.

00:01:49.724 --> 00:01:55.823
Changing the policy to five write acknowledgements
and one read response also creates overlap, but

00:01:55.823 --> 00:01:58.561
now one unavailable replica prevents writes.

00:01:58.561 --> 00:02:04.608
Neither arithmetic rule alone specifies how a reader
handles different versions, concurrent writes

00:02:04.608 --> 00:02:08.681
or a write that reached some replicas before its
client timed out.

00:02:08.681 --> 00:02:10.471
Those require protocol rules.

00:02:10.471 --> 00:02:16.518
Apply the calculation to the replica set for this
record, not to the total number of machines in a

00:02:16.518 --> 00:02:18.123
large partitioned cluster.

00:02:18.161 --> 00:02:23.337
To evaluate a consistency claim, draw when each
operation starts and finishes.

00:02:23.337 --> 00:02:28.580
A legal explanation must respect completed
operations that precede later calls.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:35.217
This small history makes the difference between
session behavior and linearizability visible without

00:02:35.217 --> 00:02:36.478
relying on slogans.

00:02:36.478 --> 00:02:39.132
A depot changes a gate code from A to B.

00:02:39.161 --> 00:02:43.030
The update completes before a different operator
begins a read.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.882
For a linearizable register with no intervening
write, that read must return B.

00:02:47.882 --> 00:02:53.777
If the read overlapped the update, returning either
value might be legal, depending on where the

00:02:53.777 --> 00:02:57.217
operations take effect within their execution
intervals.

00:02:57.217 --> 00:03:00.349
Now let both operators remain on separate replicas.

00:03:00.361 --> 00:03:06.123
Each may see an internally sensible sequence while
the second operator still reads A after the

00:03:06.123 --> 00:03:07.165
completed update.

00:03:07.165 --> 00:03:12.621
Preserving each client’s order does not enforce this
cross-client real-time relationship.

00:03:12.621 --> 00:03:18.628
A linearizable read also cannot guarantee that a
later, separate action uses current data: another

00:03:18.628 --> 00:03:20.406
write can occur between them.

00:03:20.406 --> 00:03:26.536
Protecting a combined business operation may require
a suitable transaction or conditional update in

00:03:26.536 --> 00:03:28.559
addition to clear read semantics.
